{"reply":"In Chapter 5, Alice’s changing size turns identity into something unstable rather than fixed. When the Caterpillar asks, “Who are _you?_” she connects her physical changes directly to uncertainty about the self: “I can’t explain _myself_, I’m afraid, sir,” said Alice, “because I’m not myself, you see.” Her body no longer provides a reliable sense of continuity.

The connection works in both directions: changing size makes her feel unlike herself, while feeling changed also appears through disrupted memory and language. She says, “I can’t remember things as I used—and I don’t keep the same size for ten minutes together!” The failed recitations show that even her words and memories seem altered, so the question of who she is becomes more than a question about appearance. It becomes a question about whether anything—body, memory, or language—can remain stable enough to define her.

